<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Podgorica Assembly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">November <a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica">Podgorica</a> Assembly was a gathering of elected representatives that met in Podgorica in November 1918, at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. On 26 November it deposed King Nicholas I and the Petrović-Njegoš dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and voted for unconditional union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, soon part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>. Opponents considered it illegitimate, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-uprising">Christmas Uprising</a> followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/podgorica-assembly.jpg" alt="Podgorica Assembly" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serbia1918.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, placing its army under the Royal Serbian Army's High Command by a treaty of 4 August 1914. After the Austro-Hungarian advance across Lovćen in the Montenegrin campaign, King Nikola I and most of his government left the country on 19 January 1916, while the ministers who stayed surrendered. In exile the former minister Andrija Radović, whose plan for union Nikola had rejected in January 1917, set up the Montenegrin Committee for Unification in cooperation with the Serbian government.</p><p>In the autumn of 1918 a Serbian force under Colonel Dragutin Milutinović, the Adriatic Troops, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> with orders from Prince Regent Aleksandar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> to keep Nikola from returning. On 28 October a Central Executive Committee for the Unification of Serbia and Montenegro was formed in Berane, and on 7 November it adopted rules for electing 165 delegates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The election of 19 November set two camps against each other, named after the paper of their candidate lists: the Whites, who wanted immediate and unconditional union and were financed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and the Greens, who sought a union in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> would stand equal to Serbia. The Whites won a large majority. There were allegations that the Serbian army kept supporters of independence abroad from returning, and the French authorities kept Nikola in Neuilly-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>.</p><p>The assembly met in the hall of the Tobacco Monopoly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica">Podgorica</a> on 24 November under its president, Savo Cerović. Its resolution was adopted unanimously, with three delegates absent, amid the singing of the Serbian anthem Bože pravde. Later sessions named a five-member executive committee, sent a delegation under Bishop Gavrilo Dožić to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, confiscated Nikola's property and barred his family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The union strengthened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>'s position days before the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> was proclaimed on 1 December 1918. The government-in-exile rejected the resolutions, but at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> the Serbian position prevailed with French backing, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> broke off relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> in 1920 and 1921. Some Greens later founded the Montenegrin Federalist Party in 1923.</p><p>The legitimacy of the assembly has been argued over ever since. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> regained its independence in 2006, its parliament voted in 2018 to annul the 1918 resolution, describing the union as a coup d'état carried out by an illegal and illegitimate body.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Podgorica Assembly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Serbia united with Vojvodina, and on 28 November 1918 it absorbed the Kingdom of Montenegro at the <b>Podgorica Assembly</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Podgorica Assembly</b> deposed King Nicholas I and the Petrović-Njegoš dynasty of Montenegro and voted for unconditional union with Serbia, days before the creation of the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-26">26 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1918 the <b>Podgorica Assembly</b> deposed him and voted for union with Serbia, provoking the Christmas Uprising of 1919.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-montenegro">Kingdom of Montenegro</a></li></ul></section>
